            ("fig", "first-agent-steps.webp", "Five growth stages from seedling to tree, labeled: start narrow, map the process, ground in real data, test with real users, assign an owner.", "Steps to creating a Copilot Studio agent."),
            ("p", "<b>Map the process before you build anything.</b> Write out the actual steps a human takes today, including the exceptions and edge cases. Most first agents fail not because the tool couldn't handle it, but because nobody wrote down what \"handling it\" actually meant."),
            ("p", "<b>Ground the agent in real data.</b> Connect it to the actual source of truth, whether that's a SharePoint site, a knowledge base, or a Dataverse table, rather than relying on generic model knowledge. This is where Copilot Studio's connectors earn their keep. An agent that can pull real, current information from your systems is dramatically more useful than one guessing from general training."),
            ("p", "<b>Test with the people who live in the process every day</b>, not just your own team. The person handling these requests today will find the gaps in an afternoon that you'd miss after weeks of testing on your own."),
            ("p", "<b>Plan for ownership before you launch.</b> Somebody needs to own this agent the way they'd own a piece of software, monitoring how it performs, updating it as the process changes, and retiring it if it stops earning its keep. An agent nobody owns quietly becomes technical debt."),
